.TH D_TUFT 1 "February 2026" "d_tuft 1.0" "EVM Bytecode Toolkit"
.SH NAME
d_tuft \- bidirectional EVM bytecode ↔ opcode transformer and analysis toolkit
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B d_tuft
[\-\-help] [\-\-version]
.I <command>
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
d_tuft provides CLI tools for disassembling, assembling, comparing, and analyzing
Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM) bytecode. It supports round-trip testing, function
identification, gas cost analysis, stack simulation, and function-level diffing between
contract deployments.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS disasm (alias: d)
Disassemble EVM bytecode to readable opcodes.
.RS
.TP
\fBUsage:\fR
.B d_tuft disasm <input> [\fIoptions\fR]
.TP
\fBArguments:\fR
.I <input>\: Input bytecode file path or hex string (e.g. 0x6060...)
.TP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output <file>\fR
Write output to file (defaults to stdout).
.TP
\fB\-f, \-\-format <format>\fR
Output format: \fBtext\fR (default), \fBannotated\fR, \fBjson\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-pc\fR
Omit program counter from output.
.TP
\fB\-\-gas\fR
Include gas cost per instruction.
.TP
\fB\-\-hex\fR
Include raw hex bytes per instruction.
.TP
\fB\-\-stack\fR
Run stack depth validation after disassembly. Reports max depth, final depth,
and any underflow or overflow errors detected.
.RE
.SS asm (alias: a)
Assemble opcodes to bytecode.
.RS
.TP
\fBUsage:\fR
.B d_tuft asm <input> [\fIoptions\fR]
.TP
\fBArguments:\fR
.I <input>\: Input assembly file (.evm)
.TP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output <file>\fR
Output file (default: stdout). Status messages always go to stderr.
.RE
.SS compare (alias: c)
Compare two bytecode or assembly files for exact equivalence.
.RS
.TP
\fBUsage:\fR
.B d_tuft compare <file1> <file2> [\fIoptions\fR]
.TP
\fBArguments:\fR
.I <file1>\: First input file (bytecode or assembly)
.I <file2>\: Second input file (bytecode or assembly)
.TP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-format <format>\fR
Input format: \fBauto\fR (default), \fBbytecode\fR, \fBassembly\fR.
Outputs a MATCH or MISMATCH summary box.
.RE
.SS diff
Compare two contracts at function level to detect added, removed, and modified functions.
.RS
.TP
\fBUsage:\fR
.B d_tuft diff <first> <second> [\fIoptions\fR]
.TP
\fBArguments:\fR
.I <first>\: First input (bytecode file or hex string)
.I <second>\: Second input (bytecode file or hex string)
.TP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-format <format>\fR
Output format: \fBtext\fR (default), \fBannotated\fR, \fBjson\fR.
.PP
Uses PC-bounded body comparison with jump-target normalisation to eliminate false
positives caused by code relocation and shared internal helper functions.
.RE
.SS identify (alias: id)
Identify public functions in bytecode, resolve names from an ABI, and inspect
contract structure.
.RS
.TP
\fBUsage:\fR
.B d_tuft identify <input> [\fIoptions\fR]
.TP
\fBArguments:\fR
.I <input>\: Input bytecode (hex string or file) or assembly file
.TP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-format <format>\fR
Output format: \fBtext\fR (default), \fBannotated\fR, \fBjson\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-abi <file>\fR
ABI JSON file for resolving function names from selectors.
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output <file>\fR
Write output to file (defaults to stdout).
.TP
\fB\-\-internal\fR
Also list internal/private functions (orphan JUMPDESTs not reachable from dispatcher).
.TP
\fB\-\-gas\fR
Show gas cost estimates per function, sorted by total gas descending.
.RE
.SS gas (alias: g)
Analyse static gas costs across an instruction sequence.
.RS
.TP
\fBUsage:\fR
.B d_tuft gas <input> [\fIoptions\fR]
.TP
\fBArguments:\fR
.I <input>\: Input bytecode file or hex string
.TP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-functions\fR
Break down gas per identified public function, sorted by total gas descending.
.TP
\fB\-\-top <n>\fR
Number of hotspot windows to display (default: 5).
.TP
\fB\-\-window <n>\fR
Sliding window size in instructions for hotspot detection (default: 10).
.PP
Gas costs are base costs from the Yellow Paper. Dynamic costs (memory expansion,
call stipends, SSTORE dirty/clean state) require runtime state and are not modelled.
KECCAK256 and EXP report base cost only; LOG* uses 375 + 375*topics.
.RE
.SS stack (alias: s)
Simulate EVM stack execution to track depth and detect underflow/overflow.
.RS
.TP
\fBUsage:\fR
.B d_tuft stack <input> [\fIoptions\fR]
.TP
\fBArguments:\fR
.I <input>\: Input bytecode file or hex string
.TP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-trace\fR
Print a per-instruction execution trace showing PC, opcode, operand, current depth,
delta, and a visual depth bar (e.g. \fB│││\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-limit <n>\fR
Maximum number of trace lines to display (default: 50).
.PP
Performs a linear pass. Does not follow control flow branches \(em for full CFG
traversal use \fBidentify\fR. The EVM stack maximum of 1024 items is enforced.
.RE
.SS test
Test round-trip fidelity: bytecode -> opcodes -> bytecode.
.RS
.TP
\fBUsage:\fR
.B d_tuft test <input>
.TP
\fBArguments:\fR
.I <input>\: Input bytecode (hex string or file)
.PP
Outputs a PASS or FAIL summary box. Bytes that correspond to unrecognised opcodes
are decoded as INVALID (0xfe) and will cause a FAIL on re-assembly.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Disassemble with stack validation
.nf
$ d_tuft disasm contract.bin --stack
.fi
.SS Full gas report with function breakdown
.nf
$ d_tuft gas contract.bin --functions --top 10
.fi
.SS Stack trace (first 30 instructions)
.nf
$ d_tuft stack contract.bin --trace --limit 30
.fi
.SS Identify functions with ABI and gas
.nf
$ d_tuft identify contract.bin --abi contract.abi.json --gas
.fi
.SS Compare two contract versions
.nf
$ d_tuft diff v1.bin v2.bin --format text
.fi
.SS Disassemble and write to file
.nf
$ d_tuft disasm examples/contract.bin -o contract.evm
.fi
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
.TP
\fBtext\fR
Human-readable format with optional PC, gas, and hex byte annotations.
.TP
\fBannotated\fR
Detailed box-drawing table with gas costs and full instruction details.
.TP
\fBjson\fR
Machine-readable JSON output for programmatic processing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success.
.TP
1
Error (invalid input, format mismatch, stack violation, or operation failure).
.SH NOTES
Gas analysis models static base costs only. Actual on-chain gas consumption
depends on runtime state (storage slot warmth, memory expansion, call depth, etc.)
and will differ from the estimates produced by the \fBgas\fR command.

Stack simulation performs a linear-pass analysis and does not follow conditional
branches. A clean simulation result does not guarantee the absence of stack errors
on all execution paths.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR evm (1),
.BR solc (1)
.SH AUTHOR
d_tuft was built with bun v1.3.8.
